WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.469
Reporter: The coronavirus has mutated.

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.604
Reporter: Some strains
are far more infectious

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.339
Reporter: It doesn't
prove that this new strain

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.741
is in fact more infectious.

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:10.176
Abby Tang: Hello, fellow skeptics.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.544
It's me, Abby!

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.114
There is a lot of information flying

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:16.750
into our eyes and ears
at any given moment.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.319
And it can be hard to tell sometimes

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:21.121
what's true, what's not.

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:23.757
So I grabbed three of the
most common talking points

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:25.725
that you're probably seeing on your feed,

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.695
two truths, one lie,

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:29.896
and I did a little digging.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:33.767
Truth No. 1: It's OK to go outside!

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.635
It's really nice out here.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.838
But social distancing
is still the best way

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.774
to control the spread.

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:42.242
Both things can be true.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.377
I'm a big fan of this viral blog post

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.679
by an immunologist who delightfully puts

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.182
all his credentials on the
front page of his blog.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.684
But he's very up front about
not claiming to be an expert,

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:54.621
and some of the work he cites
hasn't been peer reviewed yet,

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:57.290
so I ran it by our senior
health reporter Anna.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.025
She said, "Looks good."

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.527
The whole post is a solid read,

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.996
but the main takeaway is this:

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:05.965
Successful infection
equals exposure to virus

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.500
multiplied by time.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.437
Basically, the more viral
particles you're exposed to

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.505
and the longer you're exposed to them,

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.174
the more likely you'll be infected.

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:15.909
Sorry about this gross picture!

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.577
But this cough can apparently contain

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.280
up to 200 million virus particles,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.882
a number that I could not
find in original source for

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.084
but shows up everywhere,

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.119
so let's take it with a grain of salt.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:29.722
Point is, if a sick person
coughs right into your face,

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.358
breathes, too, but to a lesser degree,

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:35.395
your chances of getting
infected are gonna be high.

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:37.797
Which is why going over
to a friend's house...

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:38.631
not ideal.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.301
But if you just walk by
someone on the sidewalk,

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.902
chances are you're gonna be fine,

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.604
because you're limiting the amount of time

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.039
you're being exposed

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.374
and the number of particles
you're being exposed to

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.877
by not locking yourself
in a room full of them.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:53.012
So going outside is super-low risk

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.615
as long as you're staying
physically distant.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.917
I think this quote from a
previous Insider article

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:59.486
sums it up perfectly:

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.354
Erich Anderer: Outside
is better than inside,

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.656
open space is better than closed,

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.725
fewer people is better than more people,

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:07.560
and stay away from sick people.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.596
Or, better yet, stay home

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:11.297
if you're not feeling well yourself.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.565
Abby: Thanks, Erich.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.869
Truth No. 2: The virus
that causes COVID-19

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:18.037
is mutating all the time.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:19.672
It's true that viruses mutate.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.007
Viruses mutate more often

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.976
than practically any other organism.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.711
But we shouldn't be treating it as a big,

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:27.780
scary thing, which a lot of places have.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.450
Which is exactly how this
study turned into this,

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:32.685
then had to be corrected into this.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.821
First, it's worth noting that this paper

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:36.256
has not been peer reviewed,

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.625
which it does say in
the study, at the top,

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:40.393
in bright yellow, right before saying

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:41.828
it should not be reported in news media

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.296
as established information.

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.266
But peers have been informally
reviewing it on Twitter,

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.635
and at least one epidemiologist, Bill,

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:51.004
thinks the paper itself is fine.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:54.073
So, based on that, the problem
is in interpreting the data.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.643
The first headline says
that the mutant coronavirus

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:58.912
is more contagious than the original.

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:02.148
But while the mutated strain
of the virus is more prevalent,

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:04.817
we don't know if it was the
mutation that gave it a leg up.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:05.852
There are other factors,

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.420
like the fact that this particular strain

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.988
happened to show up on the East Coast,

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.791
which responded to the outbreak
more slowly than the West.

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.860
And this is something
that happens all the time,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.729
but mutation is not a dirty word.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.331
Bill put it best in his tweet:

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.934
"Mutations are what happens
when genomes replicate.

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:24.871
Comes with the territory like
showers with the springtime."

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:25.872
Poetry.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.541
And if you want to know
what mutation actually is,

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.476
I made a whole entire video about it

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.011
that you can see here.

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.347
And now the lie:

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.550
COVID-19 only kills older people

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.786
and those with preexisting conditions.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:42.455
Here's why this meme really gets my goat.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.991
The goal seems to be to quell some panic,

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:48.428
but what it really does
is spread misinformation.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.530
As far as we know at this point,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.498
anyone can die from this disease.

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:55.368
Old people, healthy people, children.

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:57.203
Yeah. Unfortunately, there have been

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.505
a couple documented cases.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.708
This study is a little on the older side,

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.376
but it's still the biggest one we have.

00:04:03.376 --> 00:04:05.378
The death rate is absolutely lower

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.946
the younger and healthier you are.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.949
But do you want to be one
of the few in that 0.2%,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.584
guy from my Facebook feed?

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:12.986
I'm gonna say no.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.320
As new information comes out

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.023
about who this virus is
most dangerous for and why,

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:19.325
the risk by population adjusts.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.227
Which is a really complicated topic,

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:23.162
so if you want to learn more about it,

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:24.631
my colleague did a video here.

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:28.034
But new information only
changes what we know about risk;

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:29.936
it doesn't eliminate it.

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.772
And, yeah, I don't want to die from this,

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.341
but I don't want to be sick either.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:38.444
This CDC data suggests that 30% of cases

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:41.047
and 20% of hospitalizations in the US

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:44.284
are people between the ages of 20 and 44.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:47.053
And recovery can be a
slow and painful process

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.488
that could take months.

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:51.257
Bottom line: Low risk
doesn't mean no risk.

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:53.726
So protect yourself and
the people around you

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.161
by being cautious.

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:57.463
Not panicky, but cautious.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:00.333
So there you go: two truths and a lie.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:02.402
Add them to your Hinge profile.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:04.003
And if any of this sets off

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:07.807
your this-is-complete-crap-ometer, great!

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.475
Stay skeptical.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:12.178
Fact-check me; my sources
are in the credits.

